{
  /* M2 note: this root tsconfig.json is Next.js's config, not lib/'s.
     `lib/` kept its original strict NodeNext config verbatim — see
     tsconfig.lib.json for why and what "npm run typecheck" now does with
     both files.

     Why this file differs from tsconfig.lib.json:
       - moduleResolution "bundler" / module "esnext" (instead of NodeNext)
         because Next.js compiles `app/**` through webpack/Turbopack, never
         through Node's own ESM loader — "bundler" is the resolution
         algorithm that actually matches how this code runs. TypeScript's
         "bundler" mode also resolves an extension-ful relative import like
         "../../lib/identity/index.js" straight to "index.ts", so the
         route handler can import lib/identity's NodeNext-style ".js"
         specifiers with no changes to lib/ at all.
       - "jsx": "react-jsx" and the "next" plugin: Next.js's own build
         step enforces this jsx mode itself (it rewrote this file to set
         it — see the mandatory-changes note `next build` prints) so its
         compiler emits the React automatic-runtime JSX, and so
         `.next/types` gets picked up for typed routes. Next.js also
         freely rewrites this file's `include` array on every build/dev
         run (e.g. adding an extra `.next/dev/types` glob entry) — that
         is expected and is not something to revert.
     What this costs: this config no longer catches the one specific class
     of mistake NodeNext resolution exists to catch inside app/** — a
     relative import that would fail to resolve under Node's own ESM
     loader (e.g. a missing ".js" extension). That check still runs, just
     narrowed to lib/ via tsconfig.lib.json, because app/** never executes
     under Node's raw ESM loader (Next.js's bundler runs it), so the
     narrower NodeNext guarantee wasn't protecting against a real failure
     mode for app code in the first place. Every strictness flag
     (`strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`,
     etc.) is kept as-is — nothing here is a loosening of type safety,
     only of which module-resolution algorithm is simulated. */
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": [
      "dom",
      "dom.iterable",
      "ES2022"
    ],
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    /* Strictness — identical to tsconfig.lib.json. Not relaxed. */
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noImplicitReturns": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "incremental": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "plugins": [
      {
        "name": "next"
      }
    ]
  },
  "include": [
    "next-env.d.ts",
    "app/**/*.ts",
    "app/**/*.tsx",
    "next.config.ts",
    ".next/types/**/*.ts",
    ".next/dev/types/**/*.ts"
  ],
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules"
  ]
}
